{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Pink Lake Summer: Halophilic Carotenoid Bloom at Lake Hillier",
    "date": "2026-10-01",
    "lesson": "Lake Hillier in Western Australia stays bubblegum-pink year round because its brine, far saltier than the sea, is dominated by halophilic microbes (Salinibacter, halophilic Archaea, Dunaliella algae) that pack their membranes with carotenoid pigments as sunscreen and osmotic protection. Almost nothing else can survive the salt, so the pigment-makers face little competition or grazing, but they pay for it: every cell must constantly pump incompatible solutes against a brutal osmotic gradient, which is why upkeep and swim cost are high even though predators are scarce. This level asks whether a founder can profit from the carotenoid trick - re-skinned here as a lipid-class pigment-protein enzyme - in a hot, saturated, nutrient-thin brine where the only food that persists is wind-blown organic flecks and dead Dunaliella.",
    "citation": "Educational simulation inspired by Lake Hillier, Western Australia; general halophile ecology (Oren 2008, salt lake microbiology)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Lake Hillier, Western Australia - a naturally pink hypersaline lake colored by halophilic carotenoid-producing microbes"
  },
